mode: "100644"
text: "# Platform Mechanics\n\nCaption rules differ on every platform: how much shows before the cutoff, how long you can go,\nwhether hashtags or links help or hurt, and what the feed rewards. Get these wrong and a good\ncaption underperforms.\n\n> **Verify before relying on exact numbers.** Platforms change limits and algorithm behavior\n> often; these are accurate as of build and should be re-checked quarterly. Treat the\n> *principles* as durable and the *numbers* as approximate.\n\n---\n\n## Instagram (feed / Reels)\n\n- **Caption limit:** ~2,200 characters.\n- **Preview cutoff:** ~125 characters before \"…more.\" The first line/sentence must hook within\n  that.\n- **Hashtags:** up to 30 allowed, but more ≠ more reach. Instagram has said hashtags help\n  *categorize*, not boost reach — use a handful (≈3–5) of genuinely relevant ones. In caption\n  or first comment both work; pick one and stay consistent.\n- **Links:** not clickable in captions → route to \"link in bio.\" Don't write a clickable-link\n  CTA.\n- **Rewards:** saves and shares (sends) are the strongest signals; comments next. Write toward\n  \"save this\" / \"send this to someone.\"\n- **Length:** flexible — short punchy or long story both work if the first line earns it. Use\n  line breaks for readability.\n\n## LinkedIn\n\n- **Limit:** ~3,000 characters.\n- **Preview cutoff:** ~140 characters on mobile before \"…see more\" (a bit more on desktop). The\n  hook line is critical; the \"…see more\" tap is the engagement you're fighting for.\n- **Hashtags:** ~3–5 relevant ones, usually at the end.\n- **Links:** external links in the post body can suppress reach; a common tactic is to put the\n  link in the first comment and say \"link in comments.\" Use judgment per the brand.\n- **Rewards:** dwell time and early comments. Whitespace and one-line paragraphs increase\n  read-through. Posts that spark genuine discussion travel.\n- **Length:** medium-to-long performs well when it earns the read; format with generous line\n  breaks.\n- Note: for dedicated thought-leadership posts, hand off to `linkedin-post-writer`.\n\n## TikTok\n\n- **Caption limit:** up to ~4,000 characters (expanded from 2,200; verify).\n- **Preview:** only a short portion shows over the video; lead with the hook or a curiosity\n  gap.\n- **Hashtags & keywords:** relevant hashtags help categorization; **keywords in the caption\n  feed TikTok search/SEO** — write for what people type, not just tags. A couple of specific\n  tags beat a pile of broad ones.\n- **Links:** not clickable in captions → \"link in bio.\"\n- **Rewards:** watch time/rewatches (the video does this) and comments. Captions that pose a\n  question or set up the video's payoff drive comments.\n- **Length:** usually short; the video carries the message. Use the caption to add context,\n  a hook, or a question.\n\n## X / Twitter\n\n- **Limit:** 280 characters (free); much longer with Premium, but short still travels.\n- **Hashtags:** 0–2, often none. More than a couple reads as spam and can hurt.\n- **Links:** fine; some believe links dampen reach (debated) — lead with the point, link after.\n- **Rewards:** replies, reposts, and bookmarks. Lead with the strongest line; the post *is* the\n  caption.\n- **Length:** short and punchy. Cut everything that isn't the point. For multi-post threads,\n  hand off to `thread-writer`.\n\n## Facebook\n\n- **Limit:** very long (~63,206 characters) — irrelevant in practice.\n- **Preview cutoff:** ~80 characters before \"See more.\" Hook fast.\n- **Hashtags:** largely ineffective; skip or use sparingly.\n- **Links:** clickable and fine.\n- **Rewards:** comments and shares; meaningful interactions. Often an older audience — adjust\n  references accordingly.\n- **Length:** short-to-medium; conversational.\n\n## Threads\n\n- **Limit:** ~500 characters per post.\n- **Tags:** one topic tag per post (not multi-hashtag culture). Keep it conversational.\n- **Links:** clickable.\n- **Rewards:** replies and conversation; casual, reactive, opinion-friendly.\n- **Length:** short and conversational; lead with the take.\n\n## Pinterest\n\n- **Pin description:** ~500 characters.\n- **SEO is the game:** keywords in the title and description drive discovery far more than\n  hashtags (which are now minor). Write descriptive, keyword-rich copy people would search.\n- **Links:** clickable — Pinterest is a genuine traffic driver; the CTA can point to the link.\n- **Rewards:** saves and outbound clicks. Write toward \"save for later\" and clear destination\n  value.\n\n## YouTube (descriptions / community posts)\n\n- **Description limit:** ~5,000 characters.\n- **Preview:** first 2–3 lines (~100 chars) show above \"…more\" — put the hook and key link/\n  keywords there.\n- **Keywords:** the description aids search/SEO; front-load the important terms naturally.\n- **Links:** clickable.\n- **Community posts:** short, conversational, image/poll-friendly.\n\n## Bluesky\n\n- **Limit:** ~300 characters.\n- **Hashtags:** supported; a few relevant ones.\n- **Links:** clickable.\n- **Rewards:** replies and reposts; casual, community-forward, less algorithmic. Lead with the\n  point.\n\n---\n\n## Quick reference\n\n| Platform | Limit | Hook cutoff | Hashtags | Links clickable |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Instagram | ~2,200 | ~125 ch | ~3–5 relevant | No (bio) |\n| LinkedIn | ~3,000 | ~140 ch | ~3–5 | Reach risk → comments |\n| TikTok | ~4,000 | short | few + keywords | No (bio) |\n| X | 280 (free) | n/a | 0–2 | Yes |\n| Facebook | huge | ~80 ch | skip | Yes |\n| Threads | ~500 | short | 1 tag | Yes |\n| Pinterest | ~500 | n/a | keywords > tags | Yes |\n| YouTube | ~5,000 | ~2–3 lines | few + keywords | Yes |\n| Bluesky | ~300 | short | few | Yes |\n"
